AU  - Jürgenson, H
ED  - Cygas, D
ED  - Froehner, KD
TI  - Estonian high precision fitted geoid Est-Geoid2003 and later developments
T2  - 6TH INTERNATIONAL CONFERENCE ENVIRONMENTAL ENGINEERING, VOLS 1 AND 2
CP  - 6th International Conference on Environmental Engineering
AB  - In this paper I focus on issues related to the calculation of a high precision geoid model on Estonian territory. Calculations of a high precision geoid have been possible by a very extensive gathering and analyze of measured gravimetric data. For instance, for the first time in Estonia I made use of the gravimetric database of the Geological Survey of Estonia with a density of five points per km. In the gravimetric geoid calculations area gravimetric data from the area between 18 degrees-30 degrees east longitude and 56 degrees-62 degrees north latitude was included. From the gravimetric and levelling data gathered, using the FFT method, a new gravimetric geoid for Estonia as an approximately 3-km net was calculated. After including in the calculation all the new gravimetric data gathered, the gravimetric geoid no longer had any tilt relative to the geometric geoid. The standard deviation between the geometric and the gravimetric geoid was only 2.1 cm. The geoid surface is more irregular in the water areas of west Estonia where additional GPS-levelling was organized. New gravimetric geoid is used also for determination the height systems difference between Finland and Estonia. The surface of the calculated gravimetric geoid was fitted by GPS-levelling points. As a result, the so-called fitted geoid, or the reference model was determined, which with maximum precision reflects the difference on Estonian territory between the normal heights of the BK77 system and the ellipsoidal heights of the basic geodetic network RGP. The model Est-Geoid2003 is evaluated using precise GPS-levelling points taken from different measurement campaigns and also on the sea by GPS tracking on the regular ferries. An evaluation of Est-Geoid2003 using I order GPS points revealed that the geoid's error (rms) remained within the 1-cm, range. Special comparisons between the new global satellite gravity models and the new gravimetric geoid have been made also.
